<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pachamama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pachamama is the earth and fertility goddess of the Andean peoples, revered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> communities since before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>. She is honoured with offerings, especially at planting and harvest, and her cult survived Spanish rule alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Ecuador's constitution of 2008 used her name in granting legal rights to nature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachamama.jpg" alt="Pachamama" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Santa Cruz Pachacuti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Cruz_Pachacuti_Yamqui_Pachamama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
